// From System.map of our own build,
// linux-riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu-6.6.36-xuantie.
const symbols = `<i># System.map — linux-…-6.6.36-xuantie</i>
t canaan_thermal_probe
t canaan_thermal_init
d __initcall__kmod_canaan_thermal__258_122_canaan_thermal_init6
d of_canaan_thermal_match

<i># …and the core it registers with</i>
T thermal_zone_device_register_with_trips
T thermal_zone_device_critical
<i># 306 thermal* symbols in total</i>`;

const tripless = `<i>// drivers/thermal/canaan_thermal.c:86</i>
data-&gt;tz = <b>thermal_tripless_zone_device_register</b>(
        …
);`;
---

<HardwareNote
  slug="thermal"
  title="The temperature is readable, and nothing acts on it"
  eyebrow="LILYGO T-Display-K230 · hardware note · read"
  dek="The sensor has a driver, the driver is built in, and the thermal core is present. Between them they implement a thermometer. No trip point exists, no governor is ever invoked, and there is no cooling device to invoke one on &mdash; so this page is a characterised gap, with four ordered steps out of it."
  description="Thermal protection on the K230: the canaan_thermal driver binds the sensor but registers a tripless zone, no K230 device tree declares thermal-zones, and there is no cpufreq driver. Temperature is readable; nothing acts on it."
  source="docs/thermal.md"
  meta={[
    { label: "Sensor", value: "tsensor@91107000<br>canaan,k230-tsensor · okay" },
    { label: "Driver", value: "canaan_thermal.c<br>CONFIG_CANAAN_THERMAL, built in" },
    { label: "Thermal core", value: "built in<br>306 thermal* symbols" },
    { label: "Trip points", value: "none<br>tripless zone, no thermal-zones node" },
    { label: "Cooling devices", value: "none<br>no cpufreq driver in this kernel" },
  ]}
>
  <HwSection kind="the question" title="Is it safe to leave this board powered and unattended?">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      A direct question, and it deserves a direct answer rather than a survey.
      <strong>The temperature is readable, and nothing acts on it.</strong> No
      throttling, no shutdown, no trip point of any kind. That is a property of the
      vendor kernel and the vendor device trees, not of anything this project
      configured or left out.
    </p>
    <p class="hw-p">
      This is a gap that has been characterised, not a fire. Nobody has yet
      measured what this part runs at, and it is entirely possible it never gets
      warm enough for any of it to matter &mdash; which is exactly why the first
      step below is a measurement and not a patch.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="what works" title="The sensor is bound, and the core is there to receive it">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      All symbol evidence on this page comes from <code>System.map</code> of our own
      build, <code>linux-riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu-6.6.36-xuantie</code> &mdash; the
      kernel that is on the card, not an upstream tree.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={symbols} />

    <p class="hw-p">
      <code>drivers/thermal/canaan_thermal.c</code> is enabled by
      <code>CONFIG_CANAAN_THERMAL</code>, which is <code>default ARCH_CANAAN</code>
      and so arrives without ever being named in <code>k230_defconfig</code>. It
      matches <code>tsensor@91107000</code>, <code>compatible = "canaan,k230-tsensor"</code>,
      which is <code>status = "okay"</code> at <code>k230.dtsi:850</code>. The
      <code>initcall6</code> symbol is the built-in registration &mdash; it is not a
      module that might fail to load.
    </p>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="One cheap thing has not been done yet">
      <p>
        On this evidence <code>/sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0/temp</code> should
        exist on the booted board. Nobody has looked. The board was powered down
        before the thought occurred, and reading that file is the first item in the
        next-steps list for exactly that reason.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="what does not" title="Three breaks in the loop, and any one of them is enough">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      A thermal response in Linux is a chain: a sensor produces a temperature, a
      zone compares it against trip points, a governor reacts to a crossed trip,
      and a cooling device is what the governor acts on. This kernel has the first
      link and the last three are missing.
    </p>

