    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={tripless} />

    <div class="hw-q">
      <div>
        <span class="num">1</span>
        <div>
          <h3>The driver registers a zone with no trip points, deliberately</h3>
          <p>
            <code>thermal_tripless_*</code> is exactly what its name says. The
            driver is not incomplete; it was written to be a thermometer. A zone
            with no trips never crosses a trip, so the thermal core never invokes a
            governor and never calls <code>thermal_zone_device_critical()</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">2</span>
        <div>
          <h3>No device tree supplies trips either</h3>
          <p>
            A <code>thermal-zones</code> node is the usual place a board declares
            <code>trip-point</code> and <code>critical</code> entries and binds a
            cooling device to them. There is no <code>thermal-zones</code> node in
            any K230 device tree in the Xuantie tree, so the gap is not closed from
            the DT side either.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">3</span>
        <div>
          <h3>There is no cooling device to bind even if a trip existed</h3>
          <p>
            The only cpufreq symbol in this kernel is
            <code>cpufreq_add_device</code>; there is no
            <code>cpufreq_register_driver</code>, so no cpufreq driver is present
            and the CPU has no DVFS states Linux can select. The conventional
            response to a passive trip &mdash; throttle the CPU &mdash; has nothing
            to act on. That leaves a critical trip forcing an orderly poweroff as
            the only meaningful protection available, and it is not configured.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="in practice" title="What that means for a board on a desk">
    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="Three statements, stated at the strength the evidence supports">
      <p>
        <strong>Nothing in software will stop this board getting hot.</strong> Any
        protection is whatever the silicon does on its own, which has not been
        characterised. The TSENSOR block may well have a hardware trip independent
        of Linux; nobody has read the datasheet on that point yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The second C908 is still running its own firmware.</strong> Whether
        RT&#8209;Smart implements a thermal response of its own is unknown and
        outside our kernel entirely. See the
        <a href={url("hardware/first-boot/")}>first-boot note</a> for why that core
        is not ours.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Powering the board down when leaving it unattended is the correct
        instinct</strong> on this evidence, rather than excessive caution. It is
        also a cheap habit, which is the main argument for it while steps 1 and 2
        below are outstanding.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="next" title="Four steps, cheapest first, and the order is the point">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Steps 1 and 2 are research. Step 3 is a small change. Step 4 should not be
      started until 1 and 2 say it is needed &mdash; and they may well say it is
      not.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-recipes">
      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">step 1 · one boot</p>
          <h3>Read the sensor</h3>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            <code>cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/temp</code> at idle, then
            again after a sustained load, and write both numbers down.
          </p>
          <p>
            This costs a single boot and tells us whether the part even runs warm.
            It may make everything below it moot, which is the best possible outcome
            for a list like this one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">step 2 · research</p>
          <h3>Find the hardware limit</h3>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            The K230 datasheet&rsquo;s operating range, and whether the TSENSOR block
            has a hardware trip of its own independent of Linux.
          </p>
          <p>
            <code>canaan_thermal.c</code> and the <code>hardlock = &lt;2&gt;</code>
            property on the DT node are the two places to look.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">step 3 · small change</p>
          <h3>Add a critical trip</h3>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            A <code>thermal-zones</code> node with a <code>critical</code> trip
            referencing the tsensor, which finally gives
            <code>thermal_zone_device_critical()</code> something to fire on.
          </p>
          <p>
            This needs step 2 first. A made-up threshold is <em>worse</em> than none,
            because it either never fires &mdash; and is then a comfort rather than a
            protection &mdash; or it shuts the board down in the middle of normal
            work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-rec">
        <div>
          <p class="k">step 4 · only if needed</p>
          <h3>Consider throttling</h3>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            Which means a cpufreq driver and an OPP table: a substantially larger
            piece of work, for a board that may simply never get hot enough to need
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Not started until steps 1 and 2 have argued for it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <div class="hw-sources">
    <p>
      Research: docs/thermal.md. Symbol evidence: System.map of
      linux-riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu-6.6.36-xuantie, this repository's own build.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kernel sources: drivers/thermal/canaan_thermal.c (line 86 is the tripless
      registration), drivers/thermal/thermal_core.c, and
      arch/riscv/boot/dts/canaan/k230.dtsi line 850 for the tsensor node.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing on this page has been observed on the board. It is read out of source
      and out of our own build's symbol table, which is enough to establish what is
      absent and not enough to say what the hardware does about it.
    </p>
  </div>
</HardwareNote>
